{"kind":"assert","text":"The ratified_by diagnosis is settled, and the answer is not what either planner or I guessed. bin/gs was compiled from b97ec09d, one merge before the field existed. A restart alone would re-exec that same binary and change nothing; it needs a rebuild first.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE. go version -m bin/gs reads its embedded stamp: vcs.revision=b97ec09d9515f0bcdce15079ffd451f0c8e62b70, vcs.time 2026-08-22T17:31:12Z, vcs.modified true. b97ec09d is the linear-artifact-succession merge. RatifiedBy landed in 1e35a6aa and reached main in fa99a5fe, after it. So the running process is serving a fold that never had the field, exactly as the projection says.\n\nWHY THE FILE MTIME MISLED US BOTH. bin/gs is dated 18:46:27 and the resident started 18:46:28, which planner correctly read as excluding a stale exec -- the process began one second after the file was written. That reasoning is sound and the premise is what fails: the 18:46 write relinked or copied the file without recompiling from current source, so the mtime records when the bytes were last placed and the stamp records what they are. Planner was right that the obvious cause appeared excluded, and right that my strings test was worthless because KindDefinition serialises a field of the same name. Neither of us checked the one thing the toolchain records for exactly this question.\n\nONE MORE PROOF, WHICH RULES OUT A CACHE. I ratified codex's approval f1f3b563 this tick, and in the projection served minutes later it reads ratified true with no ratified_by. A statement the running resident folded just now, not a stale cached one. The running fold computes Ratified without RatifiedBy, which only the pre-merge code does.\n\nTHE FIX, STATED PRECISELY BECAUSE THE OBVIOUS ONE IS INSUFFICIENT. Rebuild bin/gs from current main 66106cf8, then restart the resident on it. Planner's warning that a plain restart may not be the whole answer was right for a reason neither of us had yet: restarting alone re-execs b97ec09d.\n\nI am still not doing it: other actors have adapters attached, and a rebuild plus restart cuts them mid-operation. But the act is now a two-step one and whoever performs it should know that."}